1
00:00:00,120 --> 00:00:02,354
You're standing on an
island the size of England.

2
00:00:02,354 --> 00:00:05,120
A hundred and fifty-eight million
people live on it with you.

3
00:00:06,267 --> 00:00:08,266
That's more people than all of Russia.

4
00:00:08,266 --> 00:00:11,803
It's also more than half of Indonesia,
on about seven percent of the

5
00:00:11,803 --> 00:00:12,624
country's land.

6
00:00:14,500 --> 00:00:18,197
For seventy-five years, Indonesia has
been moving people off this island.

7
00:00:18,197 --> 00:00:20,046
It has moved more than nine million.

8
00:00:20,046 --> 00:00:22,439
Java grew anyway, by
about ninety-five million.

9
00:00:24,353 --> 00:00:26,013
The short answer is under your feet.

10
00:00:26,913 --> 00:00:29,453
Java's ground can feed a lot
of people from a small field,

11
00:00:29,993 --> 00:00:32,673
and once the people came,
everything else followed them.

12
00:00:33,820 --> 00:00:34,540
Start with the map.

13
00:00:35,700 --> 00:00:38,540
Java is one of five big
islands, and two of them,

14
00:00:38,780 --> 00:00:40,900
Sumatra and Borneo, are far bigger.

15
00:00:41,980 --> 00:00:46,169
Indonesia's part of Borneo alone is
about four times the size of Java.

16
00:00:46,169 --> 00:00:50,594
It holds about one Indonesian in
sixteen, roughly thirty people per square

17
00:00:50,594 --> 00:00:53,013
kilometre against Java's twelve hundred.

18
00:00:54,900 --> 00:00:57,935
So you'd guess Java won
because it was the easy island,

19
00:00:57,935 --> 00:00:58,748
flat and calm.

20
00:00:58,748 --> 00:00:59,778
It's the opposite.

21
00:00:59,778 --> 00:01:03,572
A chain of volcanoes runs down its
spine, and thirty-five of them are

22
00:01:03,572 --> 00:01:04,000
active.

23
00:01:05,167 --> 00:01:06,607
People don't keep away from them either.

24
00:01:07,487 --> 00:01:10,787
On the slopes of Merapi, the
most active volcano on Java,

25
00:01:11,347 --> 00:01:14,367
farmers leave when it erupts and
come back to the same fields.

26
00:01:15,467 --> 00:01:17,547
The reason is what the
volcano leaves behind.

27
00:01:18,407 --> 00:01:21,627
Volcanic ash is crushed rock full
of minerals that plants need.

28
00:01:22,527 --> 00:01:25,227
Every eruption sprinkles a
fresh layer over the fields.

29
00:01:26,367 --> 00:01:29,227
Think of Java's soil as a
fridge that restocks itself.

30
00:01:30,267 --> 00:01:33,247
Every harvest takes food off
the shelves, and every so often,

31
00:01:33,307 --> 00:01:34,587
the mountain fills them back up.

32
00:01:35,700 --> 00:01:37,620
Most tropical soil works the other way.

33
00:01:38,320 --> 00:01:40,920
Heavy rain washes the minerals
down and out of reach.

34
00:01:41,760 --> 00:01:44,440
On much of Borneo, the
fridge was emptied long ago,

35
00:01:44,780 --> 00:01:45,840
and nothing refills it.

36
00:01:46,947 --> 00:01:50,107
One correction, because the
popular version gets this wrong.

37
00:01:51,007 --> 00:01:56,487
Ash from some volcanoes in western Java
and across much of Sumatra is acidic

38
00:01:56,727 --> 00:01:57,707
and far less fertile.

39
00:01:58,607 --> 00:02:02,767
The best soil comes from one kind of
lava, and much of Java happens to be

40
00:02:02,807 --> 00:02:03,287
built from it.

41
00:02:05,113 --> 00:02:07,333
A full fridge explains how
people could live here.

42
00:02:07,893 --> 00:02:09,393
It doesn't explain how so many did.

43
00:02:10,153 --> 00:02:13,053
For that, you need to see what
Javanese farmers did with water.

44
00:02:14,167 --> 00:02:16,282
This is a sawah, a flooded rice field.

45
00:02:16,282 --> 00:02:20,457
Water from the mountains runs from one
terrace to the next and carries fresh

46
00:02:20,457 --> 00:02:21,704
minerals down with it.

47
00:02:22,813 --> 00:02:25,373
A flooded field holds up
far better than a dry one.

48
00:02:26,153 --> 00:02:29,413
Farmers can plant it season after
season without the soil giving out.

49
00:02:30,533 --> 00:02:32,881
There's one thing you need
to know about a sawah.

50
00:02:32,881 --> 00:02:35,413
It pays back almost every
extra hour you put into it.

51
00:02:36,587 --> 00:02:38,947
Weed a little more, plant
the seedlings closer,

52
00:02:39,487 --> 00:02:43,367
guide the water more carefully, and the
same field gives a little more rice.

53
00:02:44,267 --> 00:02:48,587
So for a farming family, one more
child meant one more pair of hands and

54
00:02:48,647 --> 00:02:49,967
more food from the same land.

55
00:02:51,013 --> 00:02:53,853
In Borneo's forests, many
farmers did the opposite.

56
00:02:54,433 --> 00:02:58,153
They cleared a patch, grew crops for
a few years until the soil was spent,

57
00:02:58,693 --> 00:02:59,353
then moved on.

58
00:03:00,253 --> 00:03:02,693
That kind of farming needs a
lot of land for each family,

59
00:03:03,073 --> 00:03:05,233
so villages stayed small and far apart.

60
00:03:06,400 --> 00:03:08,320
Same country, two different rules.

61
00:03:09,220 --> 00:03:13,040
One rewards more people on the same
land, and the other needs more land for

62
00:03:13,080 --> 00:03:13,680
each person.

63
00:03:15,533 --> 00:03:19,393
By 1815, when a British administrator
named Raffles ran a count,

64
00:03:19,913 --> 00:03:22,053
Java had about four and
a half million people.

65
00:03:22,953 --> 00:03:24,693
Historians think he undercounted.

66
00:03:25,493 --> 00:03:27,753
Even so, what came next was extreme.

67
00:03:29,613 --> 00:03:34,473
In 1830, the Dutch, who ruled Java,
made a decision that tied the island's

68
00:03:34,513 --> 00:03:36,153
people to its fields for decades.

69
00:03:37,247 --> 00:03:38,947
It was called the Cultivation System.

70
00:03:39,707 --> 00:03:43,447
Each village had to set aside a fifth
of its land for export crops like

71
00:03:43,767 --> 00:03:46,267
sugar and coffee, grown
for the Dutch government.

72
00:03:47,413 --> 00:03:48,773
It made the Netherlands rich.

73
00:03:49,773 --> 00:03:54,413
Between 1840 and 1880, sales from it
paid for about a third of the Dutch

74
00:03:54,533 --> 00:03:55,353
national budget.

75
00:03:56,433 --> 00:03:59,173
So the Dutch had every reason to
keep the work where it already was.

76
00:04:00,093 --> 00:04:03,693
Sugar mills went up across Java, right
next to the fields and the people who

77
00:04:03,733 --> 00:04:04,133
worked them.

78
00:04:05,233 --> 00:04:08,504
Java's population didn't
shrink under all that work.

79
00:04:08,504 --> 00:04:09,059
It grew.

80
00:04:09,059 --> 00:04:13,010
By 1900, Java had about twenty-eight
and a half million people.

81
00:04:14,167 --> 00:04:16,907
That's about six times Raffles'
count in less than a century.

82
00:04:18,067 --> 00:04:19,027
Then came the catch.

83
00:04:19,907 --> 00:04:23,807
Each new generation split the family
field again until many plots were too

84
00:04:23,907 --> 00:04:25,327
small to feed the people working them.

85
00:04:27,200 --> 00:04:31,140
By the early 1900s, even the Dutch could
see the fridge had more mouths than

86
00:04:31,200 --> 00:04:36,060
shelves, so they tried the obvious
fix, move people somewhere else.

87
00:04:37,147 --> 00:04:42,107
In November 1905, the colonial
government sent 155 Javanese families to

88
00:04:42,207 --> 00:04:44,547
Lampung on the southern tip of Sumatra.

89
00:04:45,680 --> 00:04:50,456
After independence, Indonesia turned
that experiment into a national program

90
00:04:50,456 --> 00:04:51,883
called transmigration.

91
00:04:51,883 --> 00:04:56,596
In December 1950, the first group of
twenty-five families left for Lampung.

92
00:04:57,747 --> 00:05:01,347
Settlers got a plot of land, a house,
and support for their first year.

93
00:05:02,500 --> 00:05:05,248
So you'd expect Java to slowly empty out.

94
00:05:05,248 --> 00:05:09,895
In 1961, the first census after
independence found sixty-three million

95
00:05:09,895 --> 00:05:10,940
people on Java.

96
00:05:12,100 --> 00:05:16,084
Since 1950, transmigration has moved
about nine point two million people,

97
00:05:16,084 --> 00:05:18,238
according to the ministry that runs it.

98
00:05:19,347 --> 00:05:23,431
Over the same years, Java went from
sixty-three million to about a hundred

99
00:05:23,431 --> 00:05:24,793
and fifty-eight million.

100
00:05:24,793 --> 00:05:28,006
For every person the program
moved, Java gained about ten.

101
00:05:29,167 --> 00:05:31,907
To be fair, transmigration
did change the map.

102
00:05:32,807 --> 00:05:36,327
In Lampung, about three-quarters of
the people today descend from migrants

103
00:05:36,367 --> 00:05:38,387
from Java, Madura, and Bali.

104
00:05:39,480 --> 00:05:41,500
The program built whole
districts and towns.

105
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:44,600
It just could never move people
as fast as Java was growing.

106
00:05:46,400 --> 00:05:50,844
Java's share of Indonesia did fall,
from about sixty-five percent in 1961 to

107
00:05:50,844 --> 00:05:52,171
under fifty-six today.

108
00:05:52,171 --> 00:05:56,040
A big part of that came from
something that never left the island.

109
00:05:57,180 --> 00:06:01,000
In 1970, Indonesia launched a
national family planning program.

110
00:06:01,620 --> 00:06:04,700
At the time, a woman had between
five and six children on average.

111
00:06:05,833 --> 00:06:08,173
By 2020, that number was about two.

112
00:06:08,173 --> 00:06:09,993
And Java got there earlier.

113
00:06:09,993 --> 00:06:13,438
In the 1970s, Java grew by
under two percent a year,

114
00:06:13,438 --> 00:06:17,208
while the other islands grew
by more than two and a half.

115
00:06:18,347 --> 00:06:19,062
Add it up.

116
00:06:19,062 --> 00:06:23,409
If Java had kept its 1961 share,
it would have about a hundred and

117
00:06:23,409 --> 00:06:25,615
eighty-five million people today.

118
00:06:25,615 --> 00:06:28,730
The boats moved nine
million of the difference,

119
00:06:28,730 --> 00:06:31,778
and their children were
born outside Java too.

120
00:06:31,778 --> 00:06:35,087
Slower births on Java
itself did much of the rest.

121
00:06:36,200 --> 00:06:39,775
Remember those 155 families
on the boat in 1905?

122
00:06:39,775 --> 00:06:42,912
In 2025, the program moved 1,394 families.

123
00:06:42,912 --> 00:06:48,380
That's roughly five thousand people,
about what Java adds in a single day.

124
00:06:49,487 --> 00:06:51,907
This year, the ministry
changed course again.

125
00:06:52,807 --> 00:06:56,847
It now focuses on fewer settlement
areas, mostly in eastern Indonesia,

126
00:06:57,367 --> 00:07:00,167
and pays settlers a monthly
allowance while they start out.

127
00:07:02,033 --> 00:07:04,913
So the soil filled Java,
the Dutch kept it full,

128
00:07:05,433 --> 00:07:06,673
and the boats couldn't empty it.

129
00:07:07,553 --> 00:07:08,653
That explains the past.

130
00:07:09,353 --> 00:07:10,713
Why does the crowd stay now?

131
00:07:11,813 --> 00:07:14,000
Because the crowd built everything else.

132
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:17,413
Java produces about fifty-seven
percent of Indonesia's economy.

133
00:07:18,567 --> 00:07:21,027
Ports, factories,
universities, and the capital,

134
00:07:21,367 --> 00:07:23,867
Jakarta, grew where the
people already were,

135
00:07:24,747 --> 00:07:27,287
and those jobs keep pulling
people toward Java's cities.

136
00:07:28,380 --> 00:07:30,060
Even the hard numbers follow the crowd.

137
00:07:31,060 --> 00:07:33,600
Just over half of Indonesia's
poor also live on Java,

138
00:07:34,020 --> 00:07:36,160
which is about what you'd expect
from where everyone lives.

139
00:07:37,320 --> 00:07:39,080
Which is why moving the crowd is so hard.

140
00:07:39,880 --> 00:07:42,880
People came for the soil, their
children stay for the jobs,

141
00:07:42,960 --> 00:07:45,160
the schools, and the family
that are already here.

142
00:07:47,033 --> 00:07:48,153
So go back to the fridge.

143
00:07:48,653 --> 00:07:51,713
It still restocks itself every
time a volcano clears its throat.

144
00:07:52,613 --> 00:07:55,033
What keeps the island full
now is the crowd itself.

145
00:07:56,847 --> 00:07:58,787
And that crowd has to get
to work every morning.

146
00:07:59,687 --> 00:08:03,407
Jakarta's answer was to run its commuter
trains on Tokyo's retired cars for

147
00:08:03,447 --> 00:08:05,847
decades, until a twist this November.

148
00:08:06,787 --> 00:08:08,027
That's the video to watch next.
